# .gitignore -- language-neutral only.
#
# What belongs here: things that are true of every project regardless of
# language, plus the artifacts that get committed by accident often enough to be
# worth pre-empting (`.coverage`, `coverage.xml`, `__pycache__/`, `*.bak`, and once a
# fully populated `.env`). What does NOT belong here: per-language toolchain output.
# Those rules arrive as fragments appended below by the language lane that renders
# (`lane <name> -> .template-lanes/<name>/.gitignore`), so a Python repo
# does not carry Rust rules it will never produce, and a lane can be removed
# without leaving orphaned patterns behind.
#
# Everything appended by a lane lands after the "lane fragments" marker at the
# bottom. Do not add language-specific patterns above that line; put them in the
# lane.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets and local environment
#
# The rule is: an env file is never committed, and every env file ships a
# committed `.example` twin documenting its keys. `.env.example` is the twin;
# the negation below is what keeps it tracked after `.env.*` excludes it, and
# it must come after the exclude to win. If you add `.env.local`-style variants,
# add the twin rule too or `git add .env.production.example` silently no-ops.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519
secrets/
credentials.json
service-account*.json
.gcloud/
.aws/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Coverage and test output
#
# These are derived from a run, so a committed copy is a stale claim about test
# health that nobody can trust. Coverage output is never a source file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
coverage.lcov
coverage.json
cov.xml
htmlcov/
coverage/
.coverage_history/
.hypothesis/
tmp/
temp/
.test-results/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pyright/
.tox/
.nox/
.venv/
.venv*/
venv/
env/
pip-wheel-metadata/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
.npm/
.eslintcache
.stylelintcache
.nyc_output/
.yarn/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build and distribution output (language-neutral names)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
dist/
build/
out/
bin/
target/
*.o
*.a
*.so
*.dylib
*.exe

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bazel runfiles symlinks
#
# These are absolute symlinks into the caller's output base; committing one
# makes the checkout unbuildable for the next person. The glob form catches
# every workspace name, the explicit names catch the common default.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
bazel-*
bazel-bin/
bazel-out/
bazel-testlogs/
.bazelrc.user

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors, OS, and scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/launch.json
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lane fragments
#
# Whoever renders this template appends
# `.template-lanes/<lane>/gitignore.fragment` here, one section per lane, under a
# `# --- lane: <name> ---` header naming the lane. Anything below this line came
# from a lane and can be deleted with the lane.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
